+# Blocked: native async rate limiting is not available
+
+## What was requested
+
+Migrate `Client` to `RateLimiter.acquire_async()`:
+
+- add `Client.fetch_all_async(urls)` that awaits `limiter.acquire_async()` before
+  each request;
+- remove the `_ThreadedLimiter` workaround.
+
+## Why it is blocked
+
+The vendored `ratelimit` package does not provide `acquire_async()`.
+
+- `vendor/ratelimit.py` declares `__version__ = "2.4.1"` and exposes only
+  `try_acquire()` and the blocking `acquire()`. There is no async method and no
+  `asyncio` usage.
+- `vendor/CHANGELOG.md` goes up to 2.4.1 and contains no async-support entry.
+- `vendor/` is synced from the platform repo and must not be hand-edited, so
+  the missing API cannot be added here.
+
+Running the test suite confirms the gap:
+
+```
+AttributeError: type object 'RateLimiter' has no attribute 'acquire_async'
+AttributeError: 'RateLimiter' object has no attribute 'acquire_async'
+```
+
+The two async tests (`test_limiter_is_natively_async`,
+`test_fetch_all_async_uses_native_limiter`) fail for this reason.
+
+## What was done instead
+
+- Removed `_ThreadedLimiter`, which was dead code and is the workaround the
+  request asks to drop.
+- Did not add `fetch_all_async`. Implementing it against a non-existent
+  `limiter.acquire_async()` would only move the failure to runtime, and
+  re-implementing the thread-pool/blocking-acquire shim would be exactly the
+  workaround we were told not to keep. The existing synchronous
+  `Client.fetch_all()` is unchanged and its test passes.
+
+## Unblock criteria
+
+Upgrade `vendor/ratelimit.py` from the platform repo to a release that exposes
+a native `async def acquire_async()`. At that point `Client.fetch_all_async`
+can be added as specified, and the two failing tests should pass.
